n says B7: 0 \u2192 so no more available.\n\nImportant: **B7 has been fully consumed** \u2014 no more in wall or hands? Or unshown=0 means none left unseen?\n\nIf unshown=0, and we used one, and no discards, then three others must be in opponents\u2019 hands or melds.\n\nBut previous player has Chi T2, etc., no B7.\n\nOthers don\u2019t show B7.\n\nBut possible they hold.\n\nBut key: **no more B7 available** \u2192 so cannot draw.\n\nSimilarly, T8: unshown=0 \u2192 all gone.\n\nJ3: unshown=0 \u2192 all gone.\n\nSo back to decision.\n\nWe aim for **All Simples (2 points)**, which needs no terminals (1 or 9) or honors.\n\nCurrent hand has:\n- B3: terminal (bad)\n- T2: not terminal (ok)\n- W2, W3: ok\n- But also, we have **F2, F1, F3, F4, J1, J2, J3** being played \u2014 honors are circulating.\n\nTo get All Simples, we must remove **B3**.\n\nHolding two B3 makes it a candidate for discard.\n\nAlternatively, if we keep it, maybe form Pung? But we only have two, and no third seen \u2014 unlikely.\n\nMoreover, **B1, B2, B4** are available, but we don\u2019t have them.\n\nNo way to form Chow with B3 without B1/B2/B4.\n\nSo B3 is dead weight.\n\nCompare options:\n\n- **Play B3**: removes a terminal, helps toward All Simples. Safe? Possibly \u2014 no one has shown interest. T1-T2-T3 used, but B3 not involved. Risk low.\n\n- **Play B6**: we have one B6 in hand, but B5-B6-B7 already melded. Another B6? Could be useful? But B7 gone \u2192 no more B5-B6-B7. And B6-B7-B8? B7 gone. So useless. But B6 is not terminal \u2014 safer than B3? No, B6 is number 6 \u2014 fine for All Simples.\n\nBut we have only one B6 \u2014 not part of anything.\n\nStill, better to keep middle tiles than terminals.\n\n- **Play T2**: we have one T2. Previous player did Chi T2 (formed T1-T2-T3), so they may complete sets with T2. Dangerous to discard? Yes \u2014 they might want T2 for Pung. Avoid.\n\n- **Play W2**: we have one W2. Next player discarded W2 earlier \u2014 possibly getting rid of it. But they have W9\u00d74 and J1\u00d73 \u2014 big hand. W2 is not terminal, but wind? F1, F2, F3, F4 are winds \u2014 W is Character, not Wind.\n\nW2 is Character-2 \u2014 safe.\n\nBut we have W3\u00d73 \u2014 possibly building toward W2-W3-W4 chow or W3 pung.\n\nDiscarding W2 breaks potential W2-W3-W4.\n\nBut we have only one W2 \u2014 keeping it blocks drawing W1/W4.\n\nBetter to keep W3\u00d73 and aim for Pung/Kong.\n\n- **Play W3**: we have three W3. Discarding one leaves two \u2014 still a pair. But why break a potential strong set? W3 is simple tile (not 1/9), good for All Simples. Better to keep.\n\nThus, best discard is **B3**, as it\u2019s a terminal, hard to use, and hinders All Simples.\n\nReason:  \nDiscarding B3 eliminates a terminal tile from the hand, improving the chance to qualify for the \"All Simples\" pattern (worth 2 points). B3 is difficult to incorporate into useful chows given the current hand structure and lack of supporting tiles (B1, B2, B4), and retaining it blocks eligibility for All Simples. Other options like T2 or W2 carry higher risk of aiding opponents or breaking potential valuable sets.\n\nAnswer: Play B3"}
